    "chunk_text": "PARLIAMENT OF THE DEMOCRATIC SOCIALIST REPUBLIC OF SRI LANKA\n\nCOMMERCIAL MEDIATION CENTRE OF SRI LANKA ACT, No. 44 OF 2000\n\n[Certified on 9th August, 2000]\n\nPrinted on the Order of Government\n\nPublished as a Supplement to Part 11 of the Gazette of the Democratic Socialist Republic of Sri Lanka of August 11, 2000\n\nPRINTED AT THE DEPARTMl!f,,'T OF GOVERNMENT PRINTING, SRI LANKA\n\nTO BE PURCJIASED AT THE GOVERNMENT PUBLICATIONS BUREAU, COLOM DO )\n\nCo111111ercial Mediation Centre of Sri Lanka Act, No. 44 of2000\n\n[Certified on 9th August, 2000]\n\nL. D. - 0.42/99.\n\nAN ACT TO PROVIDE FOR THE ESTABLISHMENT OF THE COMMERCIAL\n\nMEDIATION CENTER OF SRI LANKA; AND FOR M ATTERS\n\nCONNECl\"ED THEREWITH OR INCIDENTAL THERETO\n\nBE it enacted by the Parliament of the Democratic Socialist Republic of Sri Lanka as follows :-\n\n1. TI1is Act may be cited as the Commercial Mediation Shon 1i1le and Centre of Sri Lanka Act, No. 44 of 2000, and shall come into date of operation. operation on such date as may be appointed by the Minister by Order published in the Gazette.\n\nPART!\n\nCOMMERCIAL MEDIATION CENTRE OF SRI LANKA\n\n2.(I) There shall be established a Centre to be calledthe Commercial Mediation Centre of Sri Lanka (hereinafter referred to as \"the Centre\").\n\n(2) The Centre shall. by the name assigned to it bysubsection ( I), be a body corporate with perpetual succession and a common seal, and may sue and be sued in it's corporate name.\n\n(3) The head office of the Centre shall be in Colombo. TheBoard of Management of the Centre may establish such branch offices of the Centre as the Board may consider necesssary in places in Sri Lanka, outside Colombo.\n\n3.The functions of the Centre shall be -\n\n(a) to promote the wider acceptance of mediation andconciliation for the resolution and setllement ofcommercial disputes ;\n\n(b) to encourage parties to resolve commercial disputesby mediation and conciliation ;\n\nEsrablishmcnt of the Sri Lanka Commercial Mediation Centre.\n\nFunctions of the Centre."
